code_review.final_verdict:
VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED

The change is mostly clean and additive — the exception consolidation (three exception classes → one `DuplicateIdentityException`, three advice handlers → one), the new `Owner.identityKey(...)` factory living beside the other derived-value factories, and the mapper's `formatIdentityKey` sitting next to the sibling `formatXxx` methods are all idiomatic and sit in the right units. But one structural problem stands out:

**`assertIdentityIsUnique` does not use the `identityKey` it is named for — it reimplements a telephone-only comparison.**

`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicServiceImpl.java`:

```java
private void assertIdentityIsUnique(Owner owner) {
    String normalizedTelephone = Owner.normalizeTelephone(owner.getTelephone());
    ...
    boolean duplicate = ownerRepository.findAll().stream()
        .filter(existing -> !Objects.equals(existing.getId(), owner.getId()))
        .map(existing -> Owner.normalizeTelephone(existing.getTelephone()))
        .anyMatch(normalizedTelephone::equals);
```

The codebase now has a single canonical definition of identity — `Owner.identityKey(telephone, email, lastName, address)` — and the mapper, the exception message, and this method's own javadoc all point to it as *the* key. Yet the uniqueness check bypasses it and compares only the normalized telephone component. This is the exact "duplicated/partial reimplementation of logic that already has a home" smell the consolidation was meant to remove: the normalization reasoning now lives in two places, and the check diverges from the key a maintainer would expect it to be built on.

The long javadoc arguing that "the normalized telephone is therefore the decisive component" is itself a signal — it exists to explain why the method *doesn't* just compare the keys. Structurally, the method should compute `Owner.identityKey(...)` for the owner being saved and for each existing owner and compare the whole keys via `anyMatch`, exactly as the surrounding design implies:

```java
private void assertIdentityIsUnique(Owner owner) {
    String identityKey = Owner.identityKey(owner.getTelephone(), owner.getEmail(),
        owner.getLastName(), owner.getAddress());
    boolean duplicate = ownerRepository.findAll().stream()
        .filter(existing -> !Objects.equals(existing.getId(), owner.getId()))
        .map(existing -> Owner.identityKey(existing.getTelephone(), existing.getEmail(),
            existing.getLastName(), existing.getAddress()))
        .anyMatch(identityKey::equals);
    if (duplicate) {
        throw new DuplicateIdentityException(identityKey);
    }
}
```

This removes the second, partial normalization path, makes the method match its name, and keeps the single-key concept genuinely single. (The early-return `null` guard, if still wanted, should be expressed in terms of the key rather than telephone alone.)

Minor, same file/area: the `sharesHousehold` parameter on `saveOwner` is now dead — the OpenAI doc already notes it "has no effect." Leaving it as an unused parameter is defensible for API back-compat, but worth a deliberate decision rather than drift; a short comment at the `saveOwner` signature noting it is retained only for compatibility would keep the service layer honest for the next maintainer.

